{"name":"Nylon","hex":"#e9e3cb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nylon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Originally, the DuPont company trade name for polyamide, a copolymer whose molecules consist of alternating diamine and dicarboxylic acid monomers bonded together; now generically used for this type of polymer.","A stocking originally fabricated from nylon; also used generically for any long, sheer stocking worn on a woman's legs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nylon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Nylon","blocks":[{"text":"Nylon is a family of synthetic polymers characterized by amide linkages, typically connecting aliphatic or semi-aromatic groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Nylon_6_and_Nylon_6-6.svg/330px-Nylon_6_and_Nylon_6-6.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nylon","wikidata":"Q177941"},{"title":"Nylon (magazine)","blocks":[{"text":"Nylon is an American multimedia brand and publishing company, which produces a lifestyle magazine that focuses on pop culture and fashion. Its coverage includes art, beauty, music, design, celebrities, technology, and travel. Originally a print publication, it switched to an all-digital format in 2017. Its name references New York and London, and it is currently owned by the Bustle Digital Group (BDG). In 2023, the magazine announced it would return to print in 2024. It continues to release a print magazine biannually."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/de/Nylon_December_2013_January_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nylon_(magazine)","wikidata":"Q5891280"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Wallace_Carothers%2C_in_the_lab.jpg/250px-Wallace_Carothers%2C_in_the_lab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pa